Vector conversions

NED &lt;-&gt; ENU
The axes permute as: \(x_\text{ENU} = y_\text{NED}\), \(y_\text{ENU} = x_\text{NED}\), \(z_\text{ENU} = -z_\text{NED}\).
The same relation holds in reverse (the transform is its own inverse):
\[
\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}_\text{NED}
= \begin{pmatrix} y \\ x \\ -z \end{pmatrix}_\text{ENU}
\]
inline Eigen::Vector3d ned_to_enu(const Eigen::Vector3d& v) {
    return {v.y(), v.x(), -v.z()};
}
// enu_to_ned is identical (the mapping is its own inverse)


FRD &lt;-&gt; FLU
Only the lateral and vertical axes flip: \(y_\text{FLU} = -y_\text{FRD}\), \(z_\text{FLU} = -z_\text{FRD}\).
\[
\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}_\text{FLU}
= \begin{pmatrix} x \\ -y \\ -z \end{pmatrix}_\text{FRD}
\]
inline Eigen::Vector3d frd_to_flu(const Eigen::Vector3d& v) {
    return {v.x(), -v.y(), -v.z()};
}
// flu_to_frd is identical

Quaternion conversion (ENU/FLU &lt;-&gt; NED/FRD)
Converting a quaternion requires two rotations applied in sequence:

NED_ENU_Q — rotates the world frame: \(R_z(\pi/2) \cdot R_x(\pi)\)
AIRCRAFT_BASELINK_Q — rotates the body frame: \(R_x(\pi)\)

inline Eigen::Quaterniond enu_flu_to_ned_frd(const Eigen::Quaterniond& q) {
    // World-frame rotation: 90 deg yaw + 180 deg roll (ENU &lt;-&gt; NED)
    const Eigen::Quaterniond NED_ENU_Q =
        Eigen::AngleAxisd(M_PI_2, Eigen::Vector3d::UnitZ()) *
        Eigen::AngleAxisd(M_PI,   Eigen::Vector3d::UnitX());

    // Body-frame rotation: 180 deg roll (FLU &lt;-&gt; FRD)
    const Eigen::Quaterniond AIRCRAFT_BASELINK_Q =
        Eigen::AngleAxisd(M_PI, Eigen::Vector3d::UnitX());

    return (NED_ENU_Q * q) * AIRCRAFT_BASELINK_Q;
}
// ned_frd_to_enu_flu: same function (the combined rotation is its own inverse)






Warning



VehicleAttitude.q is stored as Hamilton [w, x, y, z] — not [x, y, z, w]. Match the Eigen constructor argument order accordingly: Eigen::Quaterniond q(msg-&gt;q[0], msg-&gt;q[1], msg-&gt;q[2], msg-&gt;q[3]).

Parsing VehicleOdometry
VehicleOdometry delivers all quantities in PX4 frames. Convert everything to ENU/FLU before passing to a ROS-convention controller:
void parse_odometry(
    const px4_msgs::msg::VehicleOdometry& msg,
    Eigen::Vector3d& pos_enu,
    Eigen::Quaterniond& att_enu_flu,
    Eigen::Vector3d& vel_enu,
    Eigen::Vector3d& omega_flu)
{
    // position: NED -&gt; ENU
    pos_enu = ned_to_enu({msg.position[0], msg.position[1], msg.position[2]});

    // attitude: NED/FRD -&gt; ENU/FLU
    Eigen::Quaterniond q_ned_frd(msg.q[0], msg.q[1], msg.q[2], msg.q[3]);
    att_enu_flu = ned_frd_to_enu_flu(q_ned_frd);

    // velocity: NED -&gt; ENU
    vel_enu = ned_to_enu({msg.velocity[0], msg.velocity[1], msg.velocity[2]});

    // angular velocity: FRD -&gt; FLU
    omega_flu = frd_to_flu({msg.angular_velocity[0],
                            msg.angular_velocity[1],
                            msg.angular_velocity[2]});
}
Note: VehicleAngularVelocity (from /fmu/out/vehicle_angular_velocity) also delivers in FRD — apply frd_to_flu the same way.

Publishing setpoints (controller -&gt; PX4)
Setpoints go in the opposite direction: convert from controller ENU/FLU to PX4 NED/FRD before publishing.

Thrust (scalar)
Thrust is a scalar magnitude in the controller. PX4 expects it as a negative z component in the body-NED frame (down is positive):
thrust_msg.xyz[2] = -collective_thrust;   // always negative


Torque vector
Eigen::Vector3d tau_frd = flu_to_frd(tau_flu);
torque_msg.xyz[0] = tau_frd.x();
torque_msg.xyz[1] = tau_frd.y();
torque_msg.xyz[2] = tau_frd.z();


Attitude quaternion
Eigen::Quaterniond q_ned_frd = enu_flu_to_ned_frd(q_desired);
att_msg.q_d[0] = q_ned_frd.w();
att_msg.q_d[1] = q_ned_frd.x();
att_msg.q_d[2] = q_ned_frd.y();
att_msg.q_d[3] = q_ned_frd.z();


Trajectory setpoint (position/velocity, NED)
TrajectorySetpoint positions and velocities are in NED:
traj_msg.position = {pos_ned.x(), pos_ned.y(), pos_ned.z()};
traj_msg.velocity = {vel_ned.x(), vel_ned.y(), vel_ned.z()};
traj_msg.yaw = yaw_ned;   // measured CW from North
Convert from ENU before filling: pos_ned = enu_to_ned(pos_enu).

Altitude sign
In NED, altitude increases downward (z is positive down). A drone hovering 2 m above the ground has \(z \approx -2\). This affects trajectory setpoints, height checks, and obstacle bounds:
// NED: negative z = above ground
constexpr float TAKEOFF_ALT_NED = -5.0f;   // 5 m above ground

// ENU (RViz / nav_msgs): positive z = above ground
constexpr float TAKEOFF_ALT_ENU =  5.0f;

Yaw convention
PX4 yaw is measured clockwise from North (NED convention). ROS yaw is measured counter-clockwise from East (ENU convention). To extract yaw from a quaternion in the appropriate frame:
// From NED quaternion [w, x, y, z]
float yaw_ned = std::atan2(
    2.0f * (qw * qz + qx * qy),
    1.0f - 2.0f * (qy * qy + qz * qz));

OffboardControlMode heartbeat
PX4 requires a continuous OffboardControlMode heartbeat at ~3 Hz or it exits offboard mode. The active fields must match the command type being sent:
px4_msgs::msg::OffboardControlMode msg{};
msg.position          = false;
msg.velocity          = false;
msg.acceleration      = false;
msg.body_rate         = false;

// mode 1 — attitude
msg.attitude          = true;
msg.thrust_and_torque = false;
msg.direct_actuator   = false;

// mode 2 — thrust + torque
// msg.thrust_and_torque = true; (attitude and direct_actuator = false)

// mode 3 — direct actuator
// msg.direct_actuator = true; (attitude and thrust_and_torque = false)

msg.timestamp = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;
offboard_control_mode_pub-&gt;publish(msg);
The heartbeat timer and the controller output timer are independent:
// 3 Hz heartbeat
offboard_timer = create_wall_timer(333ms, [=]() { publishOffboardControlModeMsg(); });

// 100 Hz control loop
controller_timer = create_wall_timer(10ms,  [=]() { updateControllerOutput(); });

Mode 1: Attitude setpoint
Publish VehicleAttitudeSetpoint with a desired quaternion and collective thrust. The PX4 attitude controller tracks the quaternion error.
px4_msgs::msg::VehicleAttitudeSetpoint msg{};
msg.timestamp = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;

Eigen::Quaterniond q_ned = rotateQuaternionFromToENU_NED(desired_q);
msg.q_d[0] = q_ned.w();
msg.q_d[1] = q_ned.x();
msg.q_d[2] = q_ned.y();
msg.q_d[3] = q_ned.z();

msg.thrust_body[0] = 0.0;
msg.thrust_body[1] = 0.0;
msg.thrust_body[2] = -collective_thrust;   // NED: downward = negative

attitude_setpoint_pub-&gt;publish(msg);

Mode 2: Thrust + torque setpoint
Publish separate VehicleThrustSetpoint and VehicleTorqueSetpoint messages. PX4 uses these directly — no attitude controller in the loop.
px4_msgs::msg::VehicleThrustSetpoint thrust_msg{};
px4_msgs::msg::VehicleTorqueSetpoint torque_msg{};

uint64_t ts = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;
thrust_msg.timestamp = thrust_msg.timestamp_sample = ts;
torque_msg.timestamp = torque_msg.timestamp_sample = ts;

thrust_msg.xyz[0] = 0.0;
thrust_msg.xyz[1] = 0.0;
thrust_msg.xyz[2] = -collective_thrust;   // NED sign

// Rotate torque from controller FLU frame to FRD
Eigen::Vector3d tau_frd = rotateVectorFromToFRD_FLU(torque_flu);
torque_msg.xyz[0] = tau_frd.x();
torque_msg.xyz[1] = tau_frd.y();
torque_msg.xyz[2] = tau_frd.z();

thrust_pub-&gt;publish(thrust_msg);
torque_pub-&gt;publish(torque_msg);

Mode 3: Direct actuator commands
Publish ActuatorMotors with per-motor throttles in [0, 1]. Unused slots must be NaN.
px4_msgs::msg::ActuatorMotors msg{};
msg.control = {
    (float) throttles[0], (float) throttles[1],
    (float) throttles[2], (float) throttles[3],
    std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"),
    std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1"), std::nanf("1")
};
msg.reversible_flags = 0;
msg.timestamp = msg.timestamp_sample = node-&gt;get_clock()-&gt;now().nanoseconds() / 1000;
actuator_motors_pub-&gt;publish(msg);






Warning



Lockstep must be disabled for direct actuator control in Gazebo SITL. Set lockstep: false in the model’s model.sdf and in PX4’s SITL config.
